+# Conductor Growth Experience (CGX)
+
+**Track:** `daemonset_direct_hostpath_20260327`
+**Purpose:** Log observations during implementation for continuous improvement analysis.
+
+---
+
+## Frustrations & Friction
+
+---
+
+## Patterns Observed
+
+### Good Patterns (to encode)
+
+- [2026-03-27] Design review before implementation caught a fundamental architecture issue: the initial DaemonSet track carried over the PVC pattern (tar files + init container extraction) when direct hostPath mounts are possible. User feedback on "is it fetched or already present?" led to a much cleaner design.
+
+### Anti-Patterns (to prevent)
+
+- [2026-03-27] Carrying over patterns from a different storage backend (PVC → hostPath) without questioning whether the pattern still applies. PVC needs tar because it's a shared flat namespace. HostPath is a local filesystem — use it like one.
+- [2026-03-27] TTL-based sweepers must be lifecycle-aware. A naive mtime-based sweep can delete artifacts for in-progress builds. Always check whether the owning resource (pod, container) is still active before sweeping.
+
+---
+
+## Missing Capabilities
+
+---
+
+## Insights & Suggestions
+
+### Key design decisions
+
+**Why cp -a instead of direct mount for inputs:**
+Inputs need to be writable (tasks modify input directories). Two steps reading the same output can't share a hostPath directory read-write (data corruption risk). `cp -a` gives each consumer its own writable copy. Local filesystem copy is fast (~100ms for 50MB) and avoids tar serialization overhead.
+
+**Why no cross-node cache transfer:**
+Cache data can be GBs (npm, maven, pip). Transferring over the network is slower than a cold start for most caches. Soft affinity handles the common case (same node). The rare cold start is acceptable.
+
+**Why TTL sweeper checks pods, not the DB:**
+The artifact-daemon runs on each node and doesn't have DB access. Checking for local pods via K8s API is simpler and sufficient — if a pod exists with the container handle, the build is still active.
