+# Spec: DaemonSet Direct HostPath Mounts
+
+**Track ID:** `daemonset_direct_hostpath_20260327`
+**Type:** feature
+**Depends on:** `daemonset_artifact_cache_20260326`
+
+## Overview
+
+The initial DaemonSet artifact cache implementation stores artifacts as tar files on the hostPath and uses init containers to extract them — carrying over the PVC pattern. This track reworks the DaemonSet mode to use direct hostPath directory mounts, eliminating tar/untar overhead for the local case entirely.
+
+The key insight: when the artifact is on the same node, there's no reason to serialize through tar. Outputs write directly to hostPath subdirectories, and inputs are copied (not tarred) from source directories. Caches mount directly with no copy at all.
+
+## Design
+
+### Directory layout
+
+```
+/var/concourse/artifacts/
+  steps/                    ← per-build step artifacts
+    <handle>/               ← one directory per container handle
+      <output-name>/        ← output data, written directly by step
+  caches/                   ← per-job task caches
+    <stable-key>/           ← persistent across builds, keyed by jobID+stepName+path
+```
+
+### Output volumes
+
+Step outputs mount a hostPath subdirectory directly as the output volume instead of an emptyDir. The step writes to disk with zero copy overhead.
+
+```
+hostPath: /var/concourse/artifacts/steps/<handle>/<output-name>/
+```
+
+No sidecar upload phase. No tar. The data is on the hostPath the moment the step writes it.
+
+### Input volumes (local — same node)
+
+An init container performs `cp -a` from the source step's hostPath subdirectory into this step's own hostPath subdirectory. Each consumer gets a writable copy. Fast local copy on the same disk (kernel page cache, no serialization).
+
+```
+cp -a /var/concourse/artifacts/steps/<source-handle>/<name>/ \
+      /var/concourse/artifacts/steps/<this-handle>/<input-name>/
+```
+
+### Input volumes (remote — different node)
+
+An init container fetches from the source node's artifact-daemon via HTTP. The daemon tars the source directory on-the-fly and streams it. The init container extracts into this step's own hostPath subdirectory.
+
+```
+wget -qO- http://<source-node>.<service>.<ns>:<port>/artifacts/steps/<source-handle>/<name> \
+  | tar xf - -C /var/concourse/artifacts/steps/<this-handle>/<input-name>/
+```
+
+### Cache volumes
+
+Direct hostPath mount, read-write. No copy, no init container.
+
+- **Same node (warm)**: step reads existing cache, updates in place. Zero overhead.
+- **Different node (cold)**: empty directory. Step starts fresh, populates the cache.
+- Soft affinity biases toward cache-warm nodes.
+
+### Build log visibility
+
+The ATC emits build log events indicating the fetch source for each input:
+
+```
+input "src": copying from local artifact cache
+input "config": fetching from node gke-pool-abc123
+```
+
+### Artifact-daemon changes
+
+The HTTP server now serves **directories** (tarred on-the-fly for GET) instead of pre-tarred files:
+
+- `GET /artifacts/steps/<handle>/<name>` — tar the directory on-the-fly, stream response
+- `PUT /artifacts/steps/<handle>/<name>` — receive tar stream, extract to directory (for remote uploads if needed)
+- `DELETE /artifacts/steps/<handle>/` — remove entire step directory
+- `HEAD /artifacts/steps/<handle>/<name>` — existence check
+
+### Garbage collection
+
+Three layers prevent limitless growth:
+
+**Layer 1: Reaper (primary)**
+When the Reaper destroys a container, it sends HTTP DELETE to the artifact-daemon for that container's step directory. Same as current flow, but deletes a directory tree instead of a tar file.
+
+**Layer 2: TTL sweeper (safety net, build-aware)**
+The artifact-daemon periodically scans `/steps/` for orphaned directories. A directory is eligible for sweeping only when:
+1. No pod exists on this node with a matching container handle, AND
+2. The directory's mtime exceeds the TTL (default 2h)
+
+This prevents sweeping artifacts for in-progress builds (whose pods still exist).
+
+The sweeper does NOT touch `/caches/` — caches are only cleaned by DB-driven GC.
+
+**Layer 3: DB-driven cache GC**
+When a pipeline or job is deleted, the DB marks cache volumes as "destroying". The Reaper picks these up and removes the cache hostPath directories.
+
+## Requirements
+
+1. Step outputs mount hostPath subdirectories directly (no emptyDir + tar upload).
+2. Local inputs use `cp -a` init container (writable copy, no tar).
+3. Remote inputs use HTTP GET with on-the-fly tar from artifact-daemon.
+4. Caches mount hostPath directly, read-write, no copy.
+5. Build logs show input fetch source (local vs remote + node name).
+6. TTL sweeper is build-aware (skips directories with active pods).
+7. Artifact-daemon serves directories (tar on-the-fly) instead of pre-tarred files.
+8. Existing PVC backend continues to work unchanged.
+
+## Acceptance Criteria
+
+- [ ] Step output volumes are hostPath subdirectories (not emptyDir).
+- [ ] No tar/untar for local artifact passing. `cp -a` for inputs.
+- [ ] Remote artifact fetch works via HTTP tar stream from artifact-daemon.
+- [ ] Caches are direct hostPath mounts with no copy.
+- [ ] Build log shows "copying from local artifact cache" or "fetching from node X".
+- [ ] TTL sweeper skips directories with active pods on the node.
+- [ ] Reaper cleans step directories on container destruction.
+- [ ] Cache directories persist across builds, cleaned only on job/pipeline deletion.
+- [ ] All existing PVC-mode tests pass unchanged.
+- [ ] Behavioral tests pass (artifact flow, large artifacts, symlinks, etc.).
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Disk pressure eviction (LRU) — future enhancement
+- OTel instrumentation for fetch operations — logging only for now
+- Cross-node cache transfer — cold start is acceptable
+- Compression on the HTTP transfer path
